explanation:
The structure has four parts:
- a cochain complex `cocomplex` indexed by ℕ;
- a map `ι` from the complex with A alone in degree 0 (`single₀ A`) to `cocomplex`;
- a field saying `ι` is a quasi-isomorphism. Since `single₀ A` has homology only in degree 0, this says exactly that 0 → A → I⁰ → I¹ → ⋯ is exact, i.e. A → I^• is a resolution;
- the condition that `Ext X (Iⁿ) q` is a subsingleton (trivial) for every n and every q > 0. This is precisely Hom(X, -)-acyclicity, since the right derived functors of Hom(X, -) are Ext(X, -).

The `hasHomology` field is a technical instance argument. It holds automatically in an abelian category, so it adds no real restriction.

The setting is a general abelian category with `HasExt`, which is the natural one. A notion of F-acyclic resolution for an arbitrary left exact functor F would be more general. But this definition is explicitly about `Ext X -`, as its namespace and docstring say. It is the specific notion the library needs to compare Ext with the homology of a resolution, so it is not too narrow. I searched Mathlib for declarations named with "Acyclic". The only hits are acyclic (exact) complexes and acyclic graphs. Mathlib has no notion of an F-acyclic resolution.
